Source timestamps for split key events, so the central times remote keys by
when they happened rather than when the notification arrived.

The peripheral stamps each change with its own ms uptime, both in the position
notification and in the response to a read of that characteristic, which is
what makes the read usable as a round-trip probe. The central keeps a
per-peripheral offset, estimated NTP-style as T2 - (T1 + T4) / 2 - the
peripheral's receipt and reply times collapse into one because it stamps
inside the read handler. Probes run every SPLIT_CLOCK_PROBE_INTERVAL_MS, and
immediately on connect and once the link is secured, so a re-pair re-syncs
without waiting for the next round.

The offset is per connection and reset with the slot, so a peripheral that
rebooted - which restarts its uptime - can never be timed with the previous
session's offset. Until a probe lands, and for a peer built without the
timestamp, events keep their arrival time, which is upstream's behaviour; the
consumer also rejects an event outside the last five seconds as implausible.

Error left over is link asymmetry, typically 1-2ms, instead of the transport's
latency as a constant 4-15ms bias. Both halves must be flashed together: the
notification and the read payload both grew by four bytes.

